module angle_crossings

// angle_crossings.ri — IDIOM: an angle reading of a geometric ratio is an
// explicit crossing — multiply by `1rad` to ENTER Angle, divide by `1rad` to
// LEAVE it.
//
//     let theta : Angle  = (s / r) * 1rad      // ENTER: ratio -> Angle
//     let ratio          = theta / 1rad        // LEAVE: Angle -> ratio
//     let arc   : Length = r * theta / 1rad    // s = r*theta/eta
//
// ANTI-PATTERN this replaces:
//     let theta : Angle  = s / r               // don't
//     let arc   : Length = r * theta           // don't
//     let x              = 2.5 * 1 rad         // don't (spaced literal)
//
// Neither of the first two is a style preference — both are hard errors, and
// the third does not parse. Measured with a built `reify check`:
//
//     let   theta : Angle = s / r
//         -> error: let binding 'theta' declared `Scalar[rad]` but its
//            initializer evaluates to `Real`; declared type and initializer
//            type must agree
//     param theta : Angle = s / r
//         -> error: parameter 'theta' declared `Scalar[rad]` but its
//            initializer evaluates to `Real`; declared type and initializer
//            dimension must agree
//     let   arc   : Length = r * theta
//         -> error: let binding 'arc' declared `Scalar[m]` but its initializer
//            evaluates to `Scalar[m·rad]`; declared type and initializer type
//            must agree
//     let   x = 2.5 * 1 rad
//         -> Parse error: syntax error: rad
//
// Drop the annotation on the arc-length case and the error becomes silence
// instead: `let arc = r * theta` evaluates clean to `0.005 m·rad` — a value
// that is not a Length and will not compose with one. That is the #5825
// arc-length case; the `/ 1rad` is what discharges it.
//
// ALWAYS THE NO-SPACE LITERAL FORM — `1rad`, never `1 rad`.
//
// Verified contracts:
//
//   ENTERING and LEAVING are the same one constant, eta = 1 rad, applied in
//   the two directions. `(s / r)` is dimensionless; `* 1rad` is the named
//   crossing that asserts the quotient is an arc measure. Nothing else in the
//   language will manufacture `rad` for you from a ratio — see
//   `docs/legibility/design-invariants.md`, INV-AD-1 `angle-crossings-explicit`
//   and INV-AD-2 `quotient-pure-derivative-algebra`. The named-crossing
//   catalogue is in that file under "Crossing catalogue and identities"; this
//   exemplar is the hand-rolled `.ri` row of it.
//
//   `omega` is a DIFFERENT crossing class. 2*pi*f*1rad carries 2*pi rad/cycle,
//   not eta — see "The 2π rad/cycle distinction (D4)" in the same file. There
//   is no `cycle` unit to write; the typed layer is what forces the
//   distinction, because Frequency and AngularVelocity are different types and
//   one will not silently stand in for the other.
//
//   WHY TORQUE IS N·m/rad and not N·m (#5799): same crossing. Work is
//   tau * theta, and theta carries rad, so tau must carry rad^-1 for the
//   product to close on Energy. INV-AD-1 is the general form of that argument.
//
// HONEST ABOUT ENFORCEMENT — this is enforced at an ANNOTATED binding over an
// EXPRESSION, not universally. A bare LITERAL still widens silently:
//     param theta : Angle = 2.5      -> evaluates to `2.5`, dimension erased
//     sin(<dimensionless 2.5>)       -> 0.5984721441039565, accepted
// So the annotations below are load-bearing: each one is the assertion. See
// "Enforcement honesty (D7)" in `docs/legibility/design-invariants.md` for what
// is and is not mandatory today.

structure def AngleCrossings {
    param s : Length    = 5mm
    param r : Length    = 2mm
    param f : Frequency = 50Hz

    // THE IDIOM: `* 1rad` to enter, `/ 1rad` to leave. Each annotation is an
    // assertion — remove the crossing and the binding stops compiling.
    let theta : Angle           = (s / r) * 1rad                    // 2.5 rad
    let ratio                   = theta / 1rad                      // 2.5
    let arc   : Length          = r * theta / 1rad                  // 0.005 m
    let omega : AngularVelocity = 2.0 * 3.14159265358979 * f * 1rad // 314.159265358979 rad·s^-1

    constraint r > 0mm
    constraint s > r
    constraint arc > 0mm
    constraint ratio > 1
    constraint f > 0Hz
}
